  1408                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1409                              <1> ;  The line editor (EDITOR = 1)
  1410                              <1> ;
  1411                              <1> ;  The interpreter starts here, and a program that ends comes back here.
  1412                              <1> ;  There is no prompt. It follows the spec's suggested standard for a line
  1413                              <1> ;  editor (docs/b8script.md, Environments), and is the Z80's editor, key for
  1414                              <1> ;  key. Type a line and press Return:
  1415                              <1> ;
  1416                              <1> ;    n text    put the text in as line n; the lines from n on move down. A
  1417                              <1> ;              number past the end (or 0) adds the line at the end.
  1418                              <1> ;    n         take line n out
  1419                              <1> ;    anything else is run at once, as B8Script. (Start such a line with a
  1420                              <1> ;    space if it would begin with a digit.)
  1421                              <1> ;
  1422                              <1> ;  A command key ends the line too, and takes what was typed as its argument:
  1423                              <1> ;
  1424                              <1> ;    ^G        run the program
  1425                              <1> ;    n ^L      list from line n (no number: all of it). Any key holds the
  1426                              <1> ;              list until another key is pressed; Ctrl-C ends it.
  1427                              <1> ;    n ^E      put line n in the input line; Return puts it back, changed
  1428                              <1> ;    name ^R   read the file of that name (no name: the current one: the
  1429                              <1> ;              one from the command line, or the last one typed)
  1430                              <1> ;    name ^W   write the program to that file (no name: the current one)
  1431                              <1> ;    ^N ^N     new: no program (the key twice in a row)
  1432                              <1> ;    ^V        view: the build, both stacks, and the program's size, lines
  1433                              <1> ;              and free memory
  1434                              <1> ;    ^X        leave
  1435                              <1> ;
  1436                              <1> ;  In the line: ^B and ^F, or the left and right arrow keys, move the cursor;
  1437                              <1> ;  a character goes in at the cursor; Backspace and DEL take out the one
  1438                              <1> ;  before it; Esc drops the line (after Esc it waits 5 ms for a '[': Esc [ C
  1439                              <1> ;  and Esc [ D are right and left too). Only CR, LF, BS and printing
  1440                              <1> ;  characters are sent to the terminal.
  1441                              <1> ;
  1442                              <1> ;  Lines are counted, not numbered. A typed line holds 125 characters; the
  1443                              <1> ;  one character after a line number separates it from the text and is not
  1444                              <1> ;  stored. The text is kept as typed, each line ending in CR LF, at PROG. A
  1445                              <1> ;  file is read up to its first ^Z, and a last line with no line end is
  1446                              <1> ;  given one; it is written as it is, with no padding.
  1447                              <1> ;
  1448                              <1> ;  What lasts from one line to the next: the program, the words, the
  1449                              <1> ;  variables and the data stack (TOS is kept pushed while the editor runs).
  1450                              <1> ;  An error or a break clears both stacks. The return stack starts empty for
  1451                              <1> ;  every line.
  1452                              <1> ;
  1453                              <1> ;  Registers in the editor: EBX = the cursor, EDX = the end of the line
  1454                              <1> ;  (pointers into the line buffer); EDI is free (edit resets it).
  1455                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1456                              <1> %if EDITOR
  1457                              <1> ; ^F, right arrow: one to the right, by printing the character there
  1458 000007B0 39D3                <1> right:  cmp     ebx,edx
  1459 000007B2 745F                <1>         je      ed1                     ; at the end
  1460 000007B4 8A03                <1>         mov     al,[ebx]
  1461 000007B6 43                  <1>         inc     ebx
  1462 000007B7 EB2E                <1>         jmp     short lf1
  1463                              <1> 
  1464                              <1> ; Esc: alone it drops the line. An arrow key sends Esc [ and a letter, so
  1465                              <1> ; wait about 5 ms for a second key: after a '[' read on to the letter that
  1466                              <1> ; ends the sequence, and act on C (right) and D (left).
  1467 000007B9 6A05                <1> esc:    push    5
  1468 000007BB 58                  <1>         pop     eax
  1469 000007BC E84CFEFFFF          <1>         call    waitk
  1470 000007C1 7507                <1>         jnz     .es2
  1471 000007C3 E888000000          <1>         call    fin                     ; no second key: the line is dropped
  1472 000007C8 EB32                <1>         jmp     edit
  1473 000007CA 3C5B                <1> .es2:   cmp     al,'['
